  geopolitics: "The transmission chain this file trades - Iran and the Strait of Hormuz into oil, oil into headline inflation, headline inflation into central banks that cannot ease - is now running in reverse at the oil end and unchanged at the gas end, and the split between those two is the whole FX consequence. Start with what got firmer rather than what changed. Yesterday this file corrected its own reading of the strait, replacing a transit count with a volume series, and today's reporting makes that correction better evidenced than when it was made: flows through the strait are back at roughly 82% of pre-war levels, total Middle East oil flows averaged more than 24 million barrels a day last week against 23.3 million before the war on Kpler data, and 40% of non-Iranian Gulf crude is now moving by pipeline against 17% pre-war. That last number is the important one, because it says the adjustment is structural rather than temporary - the barrels are not waiting for the chokepoint to reopen, they are routing around it, and pipeline capacity once committed does not un-commit. US military escorts are shuttling tankers to cargo ships in the Gulf of Oman. WTI is 100.691 and down 1.05%, sitting on the 100 handle, though still up 2.93% on the week, 8.74% on the month and 75.36% year to date; Brent is 114.611 and down 1.04% with the Brent-WTI spread back out to 13.92 dollars, and that spread remains the disruption signature - it tells you the residual premium is accruing to seaborne crude rather than to the landlocked US benchmark, which is why Canadian crude captures less of any oil move than Brent headlines imply. Now the part that must not be misread: none of this is de-escalation. The US Navy-led Joint Maritime Information Centre still rates the threat to merchant shipping severe, the second-highest level on its scale, attacks on tankers are continuing and Iran has claimed dozens of hits in the Gulf and the strait. A market can price a risk as manageable and be wrong about it in a single afternoon. The diplomatic read needs walking back half a step from yesterday, which is the second correction in two days on the same question and worth stating plainly. Yesterday this file retracted the Pakistan mediation channel on the strength of a Mehr denial. Today Iran's own ambassador says Tehran remains open to talks under an interim deal brokered with Pakistan and Qatar, and that Tehran has used Pakistan to offer reopening the strait if Washington lifts its naval blockade of Iranian ports - which is the opposite of the denial. Araghchi said on Sunday that Tehran is still reviewing US comments on its seven-day plan and that its conditions still stand: the strait reopens on day six and negotiations begin on day seven if the United States lifts the blockade and eases sanctions. Trump rejected that proposal when it was first floated in September but says the US is still talking. A Saudi defence minister reportedly travelled to Tehran on Monday, from a low-profile source this file cannot independently verify. Net: the channel is live and contested, not closed, and yesterday's retraction over-corrected. The gas channel did not ease and on the week it got worse. EU gas fell 1.41% to 95.18 euros per megawatt hour today - its first down day in a while - but holds gains of 11.89% on the week, 33.53% on the month and 237.98% year to date, with UK gas at 236.90 pence per therm and 220.96% higher on the year and German gas at 96.04 euros and 220.12% higher. Put the two series side by side on a weekly basis and the picture is unambiguous: crude up 2.93%, EU gas up 11.89%. The crude premium is deflating while the gas premium is still inflating. That reassigns the damage across the board and it is why today's oil move does not help everyone equally. A crude shock improves Canada's terms of trade and damages Japan's; a gas shock lands on the euro area and the United Kingdom almost exclusively. So a deflating crude premium takes the commodity leg away from the loonie - which is half of why Canada came back to plus 1 - gives something to the yen as the most energy-import-levered economy here, and leaves the euro-area and UK energy problem entirely untouched. The euro's upgrade today came from the bond market, not from energy, and that distinction matters because it is reversible in a way an energy improvement would not be. On the regime itself, the read has changed and it is worth being explicit about how. Last week was a supply and inflation shock arriving during an equity de-risking, with duration as the haven. Today risk appetite and duration rallied together: the S&P 500 closed Monday up 0.70% at 7,676.77 and the Nasdaq Composite up 0.82% at 26,169.38, ending two consecutive weekly losses on an Anthropic prospectus filing that reignited the AI trade, with the Nikkei up 1.83% at 63,325 and the DAX up 0.91% today - while the US ten-year fell to 4.9700%, the Bund to 3.3554%, the gilt to 5.3484%, the JGB to 2.7900%, the Australian ten-year to 5.1660%, the Canadian to 3.8350% and the Swiss to 0.4730%. Equities up and yields down together is an inflation premium coming out, not a growth scare and not a flight to safety. Gold is the one asset refusing to confirm either story: at 4010.97 it is down 0.14% and simply holding the 4,000 dealers treat as support, with ETF buyers stepping back before the Fed. The franc failed as a haven weeks ago and the SNB has now explained why in its own words, attributing a roughly 3% trade-weighted decline since June to carry-trade demand with little safe-haven interest - and today it lost the one residual bid it had, because French bond worries were supplying it and Moody's affirmation of France took them away. So the trade is unchanged in structure and narrower in composition: own the currency whose central bank still has an unpriced decision to make - the Aussie, with November 3 at 50-60% and a deputy governor having pre-flagged the core print that decides it - and fund it in the currency that cannot respond at all and now has no haven bid either. Keep the metals and the oil at RANGE with silver back to a small short, because the premise under all three has moved and the Fed is 37 hours away. Two invalidations, pointing opposite ways, and both are now better defined than yesterday. The first is the base case: continued rerouting and a live diplomatic channel keep unwinding the crude premium and take the oil-linked hawkish pricing with it, which is what has been happening for three sessions and what took Canada down a point. The second is the one that breaks every franc short in this file and it has not gone away - a disruption that persists long enough to convert a supply shock into a demand shock would restore the classic haven hierarchy, bid the franc and gold together, and invert most of the crosses here. The tells for it are specific: gold losing 4,000, the JMIC threat level, and a tanker loss large enough to stop the convoys. Watch the Kpler and Vortexa volume series rather than transit counts or headlines, and watch the gas series separately from the crude series, because this week they are telling different stories about the same war.",
